A||||19||Oreif||Looking for Opinions, What does everyone think?||||1||
Z||000000||Oreif||03-27-2001||10:05 PM||||Hey All, I am sending my hood to the body shop to be painted. It now has the hood vents. I was wondering what everyone thinks of this idea. I am having the hood painted black, I was thinking about having flames painted on as if they were coming out of the ducts. But I was going to have them painted in a pearlized clearcoat. That way at night or cloudy days, you would not see them but when the sun came out you would see the hue change and the flames would be visible. Sort of like "Phantom Flames"<BR>Or should I just leave the hood glossy black?<P>Here is what it looks like before going to the body shop.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Hood_sp.jpg">  <BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||03-27-2001||10:15 PM||||Looks like a sweet job on the vents.<P>As for the paint, I think staying with the black would look the best.  Personally, I just dont like flames on about any car.<P>But, if they were done right and not too big to where they were gaudy, I think they could also look cool.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||10
Z||000002||85Josh||03-27-2001||10:15 PM||||Like I said in the other thread, sounds really cool. I have wanted to do something like that since I saw the Harley-Davidson heir put dark charcoal pearl flames on a black pearl truck.<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4<BR>CAVALRY SCOUT!(stuck in Field Artillery)||||reg||1
Z||000003||fyerstarter||03-27-2001||10:30 PM||||Is your car black? I ask because my GT is white with a black hood (primer) and it has a pretty cool racy look to it. But when I get it painted its going to be all one color (yellow). So it looks allright but kinda ricey :\<P>If you car is black I would say x-nay on the ames-flay unless you are going to do something really creative. Flames are becoming so everyday its not even funny. I swear I saw an old lady on the freeway the other day in her flamed out Geo Metro :P||||reg||1
Z||000004||Pontiaddict||03-27-2001||10:35 PM||||I've seen it done and look good, but you probably don't need it. The vents should look good enough.||||reg||1
Z||000005||sqoach||03-27-2001||11:09 PM||||looks good. resembles the fieroshop's hood vents. i think you should stay with black. it's a neat idea, but i don't think you would like it after seeing it. just my 2 1/2 cents  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.JPG">  <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||14
Z||000006||Raydar||03-27-2001||11:17 PM||||I agree with the others. <BR>Your car looks so... sinister, with the solid black paint and black wheels. <BR>Of course, you gotta do what "speaks to ya'".<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||6
Z||000007||Tigger||03-28-2001||01:03 AM||||I think phantom flames, done right, from the vents would be cool.||||reg||1
Z||000008||turbo86se||03-28-2001||01:19 AM||||oreif,<BR>PLEASE send me pics of what it looks like when it's done!! That looks awesome! I've been thinking about different methods of hood vents for my turbo 86SE and I like those the best. Where did you get them? Details please!!<BR>BTW, stick with the glossy black, that's what I'd do. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=226077&a=1676320&p=20236791"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||03-28-2001||01:19 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>I agree with the others. <BR>Your car looks so... sinister, with the solid black paint and black wheels. <BR>Of course, you gotta do what "speaks to ya'".<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sinister!?! Well I guess it does. Maybe I should get a P.A. speaker and have some loud breathing blasting out of it. Then I could get plates that say "D VADER"...........Nah that's too 80's ish.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <BR>I am still debating on the flames. I think it would look great but I think it's one of those things you need to "see" to decide. Also I'd hate to pay all the extra money for the custom job then find out I don't like it.<BR>I guess I'll see what the cost is and talk with the shop first.<P>Thanks for your input everyone!||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||03-28-2001||01:28 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by turbo86se:<BR><B>oreif,<BR>PLEASE send me pics of what it looks like when it's done!! That looks awesome! I've been thinking about different methods of hood vents for my turbo 86SE and I like those the best. Where did you get them? Details please!!<BR>BTW, stick with the glossy black, that's what I'd do. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The scoops were just done that way. After seeing Archies vents and FieroShop's vent, We decided to go the same route but be different. I think it looks better with the increased angle on the sides. It seems to blend into the hood better than the straight cuts. (Actually we tried to mirror the Indy scoop angles for the face of the vents) Coyote did most of the Cutting, fitting, and fiberglassing. I'll take a few more pics so everyone can see the inside. Then when it returns from the body shop, I'll take pics of it painted and on the car.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Haze_Performance||03-28-2001||01:41 AM||||Hmmmmmmm... Well I gotta say that the scoop job looks a little cheezy.. But Ill help you out. Just drop off that bad hood and Ill give you my mint hood to try it again. NO CHARGE!!!     j/k   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I think it looks GREAT!!  I would go for the all black hood. Will the car be ready to goto Archies?<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank><BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ttttttt.gif">      </A>       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>Pennock's Fiero Forum Members Photo Page</A><BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1431213" TARGET=_blank><BR>Photopoint Gallery</A><p>[This message has been edited by Haze_Performance (edited 03-28-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000012||TwisT||03-28-2001||01:57 AM||||Nice vents!<P>I would leave out the flame job,  personally.  Flames look wicked on street rods and such,  but Fieros...   no.  Even phantom flames you couldn't see all the time.  Fieros are pure bred sports cars and need solid paint schemes.  <P>Brett||||reg||1
Z||000013||kevin||03-28-2001||02:58 AM||||Douse the flame idea. Remember the original idea of flames on early '29 hi-boys was to re-create the concept and illusion of exposive power from an engine. In a Fiero if done with the same intent the flames would eminate from the side air intake vent. The idea of flames in the front is similar to having Anna Nicole Smith wear a bra backwards!  ||||reg||1
Z||000014||mbramble||03-28-2001||10:16 AM||||Oreif,  I would be very interested in seeing more photos of the underneath and maybe a little "how-to" on how you cut the hood and went about glassing it.  I have a general idea and want to do the same to my project car.<P>Thanks,<BR>Mike<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||mrfixit58||03-28-2001||10:32 AM||||Personally, I like flames. But I think you have to consider the theme of the whole car and not draw to much attention to any one point on the car. <P>Look at the Daytona show pictures. There is a black GT with charcoal flames. The car is one of my very favorites of all time. But, he carried his theme into the cockpit with flames on his speaker using polished aluminum and stainless steel. he also had his outside mirrors, and engine grill covers polished. Combined with billit(sp?) Center Line wheels, the car is just fantastic. It also won "Best non-Stock Paint" this year.<P>If you just plan on using phantom flames in the clearcoat, leave the hood black and finish the whole car. Then, take some time to see how you can balance to flames. May, along with the hood vents you should consider phantom flames out the engine vent covers. But, beware of "Fiero flame" jokes. <P>Just some food for thought,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000016||FieroLisa||03-28-2001||10:35 AM||||I agree with Roy, I absolutely loved that black Fiero with the charcoal flames, I have pics from it last year and this year.<P>But it's up to you, and how well it would be done. ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Oreif||03-28-2001||10:48 AM||||The actual vents and sides were cut out of wood. The hood was then cut and the wood insert was installed. Then it was all wrapped in fiberglass. The cut part of the hood was just pushed down to insert the vent.<P>Coyote did the cutting and fiberglass. Basically you can do any shape or design you would like. Just make a mold out of wood and glass it in. I came up with a few different idea's like you could do 8 circles instead of 2 ovals, you could cut in 4 small ovals, or if you really want to get fancy you could do 2 teardrop shaped holes. I was thinking of trying the teardrop design on my old hood once the new one is done. Basically the center of the vent area would still be attached and level but you would have 2 teardrop holes with the large end pointing outward. I think it would look cool but we'll see. Coyote has more experience doing the fiberglassing than I but with my old hood I plan on experimenting with other designs and shapes. If I ever come up with a design that looks really outstanding, I may switch hoods again.<P>I think I'll douse the flame idea for now. (pun intended  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  )<P>Here are the pics of the bottom side. <BR>Note: There is still a lot of finishing work to make everything smooth and paintable.<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Hood_DT.jpg">   <P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">   <P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 03-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||fotofrank||03-28-2001||02:26 PM|||| So just use an outline of flames in a dark color(gray) with a lighter outline (light gray). The more flames the better. Also try using photoshop etc. to see if you like them and what style to use. <P><BR> Anna Nicole Smith with a bra on backwards! Can I watch, can I watch, can I watch? Oops,<BR>too late! <P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html||||reg||7
Z||000019||Oreif||03-29-2001||11:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fotofrank:<BR><B> So just use an outline of flames in a dark color(gray) with a lighter outline (light gray). The more flames the better. Also try using photoshop etc. to see if you like them and what style to use. <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I played around using photoshop, I'm still undecided about having it done. It looks good on a drawing, but still am unsure. I'm going to have the hood painted black and then I'll take pic's and play with photoshop more. I figure it's less expensive to not have the flames done until I'm 100% sure than to do them and if I don't like them repaint the hood. Besides, once this hood is done, I'll be experimenting with my old hood. I could always put the flames on that one then just swap hoods and see what I think!<P>||||reg||1
